*6(c) Write a shell script that deletes all lines containing a specified word in one or more files supplied as arguments to it*

*Steps:*
1) Open terminal
2) Create the script - nano http://exp6c.sh
3) Enter:
#!/bin/bash
read -p "Enter word to delete: " word
for file in "$@"
do
  sed -i "/$word/d" "$file"
done
echo "Lines containing {$word} have been deleted"
4) Save the file using Ctrl+O -> Enter and exit using Ctrl+X
5) Give execution permission -> chmod +x http://exp6c.sh
6) Create a sample file -> nano http://file1.txt - Add some lines containing the word you want to delete - SAVE and Exit
7) Execute script by providing one or more filenames -> ./exp6c.sh http://file1.txt http://file2.txt
8) Enter the word when prompted
9) Check the modified file -> cat http://file1.txt

*Result:* Successfully deleted all lines containing the specified word from one or more files supplied as arguments
